@History
$Commands History (Alt+F8)
    The commands history shows the list of previously executed commands.
Besides the cursor keys, the following keyboard shortcuts are available:

  Paste selected command into command line          Enter
  (Left-click acts the same)
  Cancel the dialog                                 Esc, F10

  Toggle substring / prefix incremental search      F2
  Show or hide the command folder column            Ctrl+F2
  Insert the command folder into the command line   Ctrl+Shift+Enter
  Go to the command folder on the active panel      Ctrl+PgDn
  Type any character to filter the visible entries
  Backspace to remove the last filter character

  Delete the current history item                   Shift+Del
  Clear the entire commands history (confirmed)     Del

  Copy the selected command to the clipboard        Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Ins

@HistoryViewEdit
$Viewer and Editor History (Alt+F11)
    This history lists files opened in the internal viewer or editor.
Enter repeats the most recent action for that file; the mode is shown in the
right-hand column.

  Repeat the stored View/Edit action                 Enter
  Open in the viewer                                 F3
  Open in the editor                                 F4
  Toggle substring / prefix incremental search      F2
  Delete the current history item                   Shift+Del
  Clear the entire history (confirmed)              Del
  Copy the selected path to the clipboard           Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Ins
  Cancel the dialog                                 Esc, F10

@HistoryFolders
$Folders History (Alt+F12)
    The folders history shows the list of previously visited directories.
Besides the cursor keys, the following keyboard shortcuts are available:

  Go to selected folder on the active panel         Enter
  (Left-click acts the same)
  Go to selected folder on the passive panel        Shift+Enter
  Insert selected path into the command line        Ctrl+Enter
  Cancel the dialog                                 Esc, F10

  Toggle substring / prefix incremental search      F2
  Type any character to filter the visible entries
  Backspace to remove the last filter character

  Delete the current history item                   Shift+Del
  Clear the entire folders history (confirmed)      Del
  Remove entries with non-existing paths (confirmed) Ctrl+R

  Copy the selected path to the clipboard           Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Ins

@MediaInfo
$MediaInfo plugin
    The built-in MediaInfo plugin reads common media containers directly
through the active file system, including remote VFS panels. It is written in
pure Go and does not require CGO or the native MediaInfo library.

  F11, Media information   Analyze the file under the cursor
  Ctrl+Q                   Show a fast bounded media preview
  MediaInfo:path           Analyze a path from the command line
  F4 in the report         Open an unsaved .MediaInfo.txt report in the editor

The command prefix, Quick View provider, report language, Inform-compatible
template, and default editor mode are configured under Options | Plugin
configuration | MediaInfo. Quick View deliberately leaves images and text
subtitle files to their native preview providers.

@EnvironmentManager
$Environment Manager plugin
    Environment Manager keeps ordered sets of process variables. Enabled
profiles are applied from top to bottom; later values override earlier ones.
An empty value removes a variable. The resulting environment is used by f4,
future child processes, and idle local workspace shells. Remote shells are
never changed.

  F11, Environment Manager   Open the profile list
  Ins / Del / F4 / F5        Add, delete, edit, or duplicate a profile
  Space / + / -              Toggle, enable, or disable a profile
  Ctrl+Up / Ctrl+Down        Reorder profiles
  Ctrl+Ins / Shift+Del       Copy or cut a profile
  Shift+Ins                  Paste a profile
  Alt+F4                     Always edit the profile in f4's editor
  Shift+F3 / Shift+F4        View or edit the complete process environment
  F2                         Configure ignored variables and editor preference

On Windows, the configuration dialog can import the Environment Manager data
stored by Far Manager 3. It checks the 64-bit and 32-bit registry views and
offers to append the imported profiles or replace the current list. The Far
registry is only read and is never modified.

An ampersand before a character in a profile name assigns that manager hotkey;
use && for a literal ampersand. Moving an edge profile again with Ctrl+Up or
Ctrl+Down creates a group separator. The Always use editor setting makes plain
F4 use f4's editor too.

Use %NAME% to refer to a value produced by the baseline or an earlier profile.
Unix-like systems also accept $NAME and ${NAME}. Use %% and $$ for literal
markers. Profile data is stored as clear text and is not a secrets vault.

Command-line operations:

  envman:                    Open without the external-change prompt
  envman:+Name/-Name/*Name  Enable, disable, or toggle the first exact match
  envman:<file / >file      Import or export NAME=value text
  envman:e                   Edit the complete managed environment

Changes made only inside a child shell cannot propagate back into f4. On a
normal F11 open, an unmanaged-change prompt can restore the profile target,
import the delta as an enabled profile, ignore its names, or cancel.

Macros can call f4.envman, envman, 4D766E45, or 0x4D766E45 with one profile or
file operation string. Opening the manager or full editor is intentionally not
supported from a macro call.

@MouseWheel
$Mouse wheel settings

Options - Mouse wheel settings opens a dialog that controls how many lines
one wheel notch scrolls, separately for the file panels, the editor, the
viewer, menus, and tables and lists; up and down are set independently.

A value of 0 follows the operating system's wheel setting (this is the
default). Any positive value overrides it and scrolls exactly that many
lines per notch.

Other scrolling surfaces (the help viewer, multi-line edits in dialogs)
always follow the system setting.
